  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{7}{3x-1}-\\dfrac{4}{x+1}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=-9$ or $x=\\dfrac{5}{3}$.\n\nClear the denominators, then factorise the resulting quadratic.",
      "worked": "The denominators require $x\\ne\\dfrac{1}{3},-1$. Multiply both sides by $4(3x-1)(x+1)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}28(x+1)-16(3x-1)&=(3x-1)(x+1)\\\\28x+28-48x+16&=3x^2+2x-1\\\\-20x+44&=3x^2+2x-1\\\\3x^2+22x-45&=0\\\\3x^2+27x-5x-45&=0\\\\3x(x+9)-5(x+9)&=0\\\\(3x-5)(x+9)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nBy the null factor law, $3x-5=0$ or $x+9=0$, so $x=\\dfrac{5}{3}$ or $x=-9$\n\nBoth satisfy the denominator restrictions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 72
  },
